const body = `<h1>7.1 — Tilemaps: The Level Editor</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    You are standing in a cave you did not build. The Platformer scene already holds a real
    cave — a floor, walls, and a way out on the left-hand wall — painted onto a grid before
    you ever opened the project. And you cannot add a single rock to it, because you have no
    paint tray and no idea where the art lives. Today you find the art, load the palette, and
    take ownership of a level that is already half-built. There is no script in this lesson:
    the editor is where a level actually lives, and today the editor is the work.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I build a level out of tiles?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('7.1', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: Extending Someone Else's Cave</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Open <code>Assets/_Scenes/Platformer.unity</code> and press Play. You can walk, you can
        jump, you stand on rock. Now try to <em>change</em> that rock. Select things in the
        Hierarchy until you find what the floor is made of. You will land on a <code>Grid</code>
        with <strong>three</strong> children under it — and the middle one is the reason this
        hour has a Bug Hunt.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Object</th><th>What it is for</th><th>What it already has</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Grid</code></td>
            <td>The snapping contract. Every child tilemap snaps to its cell size, and every system built on top of the level inherits that spacing.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Cell Size of 1 by 1</strong>, and three tilemap children.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Collision</code> (child)</td>
            <td>The rock you stand on. <strong>75 tiles</strong>, filling a box roughly 16 tiles wide and 10 tall — a cave shape, not a solid rectangle.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Tilemap Collider 2D</strong>, a <strong>Composite Collider 2D</strong>, a <strong>Rigidbody 2D</strong> whose Body Type is <strong>Static</strong>, and the tag <code>Ground</code>. Physics already works. Leave all of it alone.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Cave Exit</code> (child)</td>
            <td>The way out. <strong>Two tiles</strong>, at the far left of the floor — the smallest solid thing in the scene, and the one everybody forgets.</td>
            <td>The <em>same three components</em> as the floor, and <strong>Untagged</strong>, because it was built for a different job: <code>SceneSwitch.cs</code> sits on it and sends you to the Overworld when you touch it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>No Collision</code> (child)</td>
            <td>Pure picture. Backdrop, decoration, anything the player should see but never bump into. It holds <strong>85 tiles</strong> — more than the floor does.</td>
            <td>A <strong>Tilemap Renderer</strong>, and nothing else. No collider of any kind, so nothing here can ever stop you.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        So the level editor already exists and the physics already works. What is missing is
        everything <em>you</em> need to keep building: the <strong>palette</strong> — the tray of
        tiles you paint from — and the location of the art it is made of. Open
        <code>Window &rarr; 2D &rarr; Tile Palette</code>. You get an empty window. Now go find
        the art.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Where the cave art actually lives.</strong> Everything this project owns for the
        cave sits under <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/</code>:
        <ul>
            <li><code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/CavesTileSet/</code> — the cave, already sliced into individual <strong>Tile assets</strong> (each one a small <code>.asset</code> file paired with a sprite). <strong>1,829</strong> of them.</li>
            <li><code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/CaveEntranceTileSet/</code> — the cave-mouth pieces, sliced the same way. <strong>250</strong> of them.</li>
            <li><code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/Caves.prefab</code> and <code>CavesEntrance.prefab</code> — these two are <strong>Tile Palettes</strong>, saved as prefabs. The tiles are already arranged in a grid inside them, and each one carries a <code>Palette Settings</code> component that says so.</li>
            <li><code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/MainLev2.0.png</code> and <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/OriginalFiles/</code> — the raw source sheets, for when you need to re-slice.</li>
        </ul>
        There is no <code>Assets/Sprites</code> folder and no <code>Assets/Tilemaps</code> folder in
        this project. If you have been told otherwise, that was for a different project.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Why a Grid and Not a Pile of Sprites?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Before today every platform in your level was placed by hand — a sprite dragged in and its Transform typed one number at a time. Name the two things you had to do by hand in that world that the Grid now does for you.</li>
            <li>A tilemap stores your level as a grid of numbers plus a table of what each number means. What does that buy you that a scene full of loose sprites cannot offer? Think about file size, and think about how a program could read your level.</li>
            <li>The scene already has <em>three</em> tilemaps under one Grid, and two of them are solid while the third is only picture. Why would a designer bother splitting one cave into three grids instead of painting everything into one?</li>
            <li>The <code>Collision</code> tilemap carries the tag <code>Ground</code>. Tags belong to GameObjects, not to tiles. What does that tell you about how physics and scripting see a whole tilemap — as many objects, or as one?</li>
            <li>If the Grid's cell size were 0.5 instead of 1, what would happen to a cave painted at 1 tile per cell? Predict it, then read the Grid component in the Inspector to check the real number.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        A grid of numbers plus a legend is one of computing's oldest ideas. Read each of these
        for what it does <em>in isolation</em>, then work out what it has to do with a cave.
        None of these is from a video game.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A subway map's legend, in a route-planning app:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The map file stores "3" at every station cell.
// Somewhere else, one table says: 3 means "interchange".</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A spreadsheet cell reference on a warehouse inventory sheet:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>string cell = ColumnLetter(col) + row.ToString();
// R4 is one address. The value at R4 is the whole content.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — A printing press's letterpress tray:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The page is a grid of slots. The tray holds one physical
// block per character, and the press stamps the same block
// wherever the grid asks for it.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A hospital's bed-occupancy board (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// A ward is a grid. "Occupied" is a value in a cell, not a
// separate object standing in the room.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A theatre's lighting desk (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Two grids can cover the same stage: one for the lights that
// cast shadows, one for the lanterns the audience only sees.</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Predict Before You Paint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>In the subway example, one number means "interchange". In your tilemap, what plays the role of that number, and where is the table that says what it means?</li>
            <li>The letterpress tray holds one block per character and stamps it in many places. What is the equivalent of the tray in the Tile Palette window, and what is the equivalent of the page?</li>
            <li>Example 5 describes two grids over one stage — one for shadow-casting lights, one for decorative lanterns. The Platformer scene has <em>three</em> over one cave. Which of the three is the shadow-casting grid, and which are the lanterns?</li>
            <li>A ward board stores "occupied" as a value in a cell rather than as a separate object standing in the room. If your level were one object per tile, what would happen to the scene file after you painted a 75-tile floor?</li>
            <li>If a tile is a number in a grid, what happens to everything painted with that number when you later change what the number means?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Load the Palette and Extend the Cave</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — open the paint tray.</strong> In
        <code>Window &rarr; 2D &rarr; Tile Palette</code>, use the palette selector to open the
        <code>Caves</code> palette that ships in
        <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/</code>. Tiles should fill the window. If you
        would rather build your own tray — or the shipped one is arranged confusingly — create a
        new palette and drag the <strong>Tile assets</strong> out of
        <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/CavesTileSet/</code> into it. Drag the
        <code>.asset</code> tiles, not the raw PNGs: the slicing is already done for you, and
        re-slicing a finished sheet is a job you should only take on deliberately. Save a palette
        you make yourself next to the art it uses, in
        <code>Assets/Artwork/Platformer/Sliced/</code>, so it travels with the project.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — choose the right grid.</strong> Select <code>Collision</code> in the
        Hierarchy before you paint rock. Select <code>No Collision</code> before you paint
        backdrop. Watch the Hierarchy highlight to confirm which one has focus — painting into
        the wrong tilemap is the single most common first-hour mistake in this unit, and it
        produces a picture that lies to you.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — learn the five tools.</strong> The Palette window gives you Paint (drag
        to draw), Rect (fill a rectangle), Fill (flood a region), Erase, and the Eye Dropper
        (grab the tile already under your cursor). Paint a 6-tile-wide ledge jutting out of an
        existing wall, and a two-tile pit floor below it. Then erase half of what you drew and
        redraw it — the point is the hand, not the level.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — paint something and prove it is real.</strong> Extend the floor to the
        right by ten tiles and press Play. Walk onto your new floor. If you can stand on it, you
        painted on <code>Collision</code> or <code>Cave Exit</code>. If you fall straight through
        it, you painted on the third grid and now know exactly what "No Collision" means.
    </p>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>There is no code in this lesson, and that is not an oversight.</strong> Every
        build lesson before today ended with a file to type. This one does not, because a tilemap
        level <em>is</em> editor configuration: a grid, a palette, and a paint stroke. The rung
        for today is <strong>Outline only</strong> for exactly that reason — the specification
        below names the decisions and stops, and the windows are where you make them. Do not go
        looking for a script to write in this hour.
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: before you touch the palette.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which of the three tilemaps must a tile go into for the player to stand on it? Which one is only ever seen?</li>
            <li>What is the Grid's cell size, and what does every sprite in the cave tileset have in common with that number? (Read the Grid component. Then look at a sprite's Pixels Per Unit in its import settings, if you want the long answer.)</li>
            <li>If a ledge must be exactly two tiles above a floor so your <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a> jump clears it, and the grid is 1 unit per cell, how high is that in world units?</li>
            <li>Where does a palette you build yourself belong, and why there rather than anywhere else in the project?</li>
            <li>What is the difference between a Tile asset and the sprite it points at? Which one do you drag into a palette, and which one do you edit when you want to change the picture?</li>
            <li>If you delete a Tile asset that a palette uses, what breaks — the palette, the painted tiles, or both? Predict, then test it on a copy.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The grid is a contract, not a suggestion — and it is already signed.</strong>
        Your collider, your jump arc, your turret placement and your ground check from
        <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> were all tuned against <strong>1 unit per cell</strong>. Every
        tile you paint must land on a cell, and every distance you design must be a whole number
        of cells. When a layout fights the grid — a gap that wants to be 1.5 tiles — you do not
        resize the grid. You move the gap. Break that rule and you will spend an evening
        debugging jumps that are <em>almost</em> possible.
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: what you are extending.</strong> Read
        <code>Collision</code>'s existing components before you add a tile to it, and answer:
        <ul>
            <li>What does <strong>Tilemap Collider 2D</strong> build, and how many of those exist right now for a 75-tile cave?</li>
            <li><strong>Composite Collider 2D</strong> sits beside it on the same object. What is it for, and what happens if you delete it? (You will study this properly in <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a> — for now, note that it is load-bearing and leave it alone.)</li>
            <li>The tilemap also carries a Rigidbody 2D. Why would a floor that never moves need a body at all? What Body Type is it set to, and what does the Inspector do to the Gravity Scale field as a result?</li>
            <li>When you paint one more tile of rock, which of those three components notices, and when — immediately, or on the next Play?</li>
            <li>There is a third solid object under this Grid that is not tagged <code>Ground</code>. Find it, and say what it is for. Why is it the one a check written against a tag would get wrong?</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

