<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt: Editor Faults</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Paint a wall on the <code>No Collision</code> tilemap and press Play. It looks solid. You walk straight through it. Which component is missing from that object, and what does this prove about how much of a level is picture versus physics?</li>
            <li>Paint one tile with the Grid selected instead of a tilemap child. Does the tile appear? Where does it go? Explain why selecting the parent is not the same as selecting the child.</li>
            <li>Paint on <code>Collision</code>, press Play, stop, and look again. Did your edit survive? Now save the scene, change a tile, and <em>do not</em> save, then press Play and stop. What is the rule you just discovered about edits made during Play Mode?</li>
            <li>Zoom in and try to place a tile half a cell off. What does the editor refuse to let you do, and why is that refusal the entire value of a grid?</li>
            <li>Erase a tile from the middle of the cave floor and press Play. Where does the player end up? Which is more expensive to find later — a missing tile now, or a missing tile during a playtest?</li>
            <li>Drag a raw PNG into a palette instead of a Tile asset. Look at the result. What did you lose compared to the tiles that were already sliced?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Thinking in Reusable Shapes</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Build a small vocabulary of shapes and reuse each one twice at different sizes: a <em>step</em> (one ledge, one tile higher), a <em>teeth</em> (two ledges, one gap between them), a <em>shaft</em> (a vertical gap two tiles wide), a <em>room</em> (an enclosed space with exactly two openings at different heights). Why do designers work in reusable phrases rather than one-off layouts?</li>
            <li>Paint the same <em>teeth</em> shape once with a 2-tile gap and once with a 3-tile gap. Jump both with the character you built in <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a>. Which one is a gift and which one is a demand? Write down the number that decides it.</li>
            <li>Paint a <em>room</em> whose only opening is three tiles above the floor — reachable only with the double jump from <a href="6.4.html">6.4</a>. This is a soft gate made of pure geometry, with no code at all. What does the player learn the moment they see it?</li>
            <li>Paint a backdrop behind a room on <code>No Collision</code> using a different region of the tileset. Does the player read the room differently? Take a screenshot of both versions before you answer.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Say where the cave art lives, and name the two palette prefabs that ship with it</li>
            <li>Load a palette, and describe the difference between a Tile asset and a sprite</li>
            <li>Name all three tilemaps under the Grid, say which two are solid and which is only picture, and prove it by painting into each</li>
            <li>Name all three components already sitting on <code>Collision</code> and what each is for</li>
            <li>Explain grid-snap as a contract with jumps, colliders and turrets</li>
            <li>Build the four shapes — step, teeth, shaft, room — and say what each one asks of the player</li>
            <li>Break this task into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
            <li>Say which earlier lesson each part of this task came from, and where you would reread it</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

